homa_hijack_gro_receive: read the TCP header the GRO way before deciding

homa_skb_hijacked() reads flags and urgent through skb_transport_header(),
but in the GRO path the headers of a large packet may still be in a page
fragment (frag0), not the linear part of the skb: virtio_net with
mergeable buffers copies only the Ethernet header into the linear area for
packets larger than GOOD_COPY_LEN. The check then reads bytes past the
linear data, the packet is not recognized as Homa and goes to TCP, which
drops it (SKB_DROP_REASON_TCP_RESET: the hijack flags include RST). Short
packets are copied whole into the linear area, so control packets and
short messages work; every full-size DATA packet is lost.

Read the header with skb_gro_header() for the decision, then pull the
Homa header into the linear part before ip_hdr() is modified, as
homa_gro_receive() does for native Homa packets.

--- a/homa_hijack.c	2026-10-14 16:12:17.656523415 -0400
+++ b/homa_hijack.c	2026-10-14 16:12:17.710377548 -0400
@@ -76,10 +76,25 @@
 struct sk_buff *homa_hijack_gro_receive(struct list_head *held_list,
 					struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
+	unsigned int off = skb_gro_offset(skb);
+	struct homa_common_hdr *h;
+
 	// tt_record4("homa_hijack_gro_receive got type 0x%x, flags 0x%x, "
 	//		"urgent 0x%x, id %d", h->type, h->flags,
 	//		ntohs(h->urgent), homa_local_id(h->sender_id));
-	if (!homa_skb_hijacked(skb))
+
+	/* The header may still be in frag0 rather than in the linear part
+	 * of the skb, where skb_transport_header() (and so
+	 * homa_skb_hijacked()) would look: read it the GRO way.
+	 */
+	h = skb_gro_header(skb, off + offsetofend(struct homa_common_hdr,
+						  urgent), off);
+	if (!h || h->flags != HOMA_HIJACK_FLAGS ||
+	    h->urgent != htons(HOMA_HIJACK_URGENT))
+		return tcp_net_offload->callbacks.gro_receive(held_list, skb);
+
+	/* A Homa packet: make its headers addressable for ip_hdr() below. */
+	if (!homa_make_header_avl(skb))
 		return tcp_net_offload->callbacks.gro_receive(held_list, skb);
 
